---
title: "Comparing wsMed and MEMORE"
output: rmarkdown::html_vignette
vignette: >
  %\VignetteIndexEntry{Comparing wsMed and MEMORE}
  %\VignetteEngine{knitr::rmarkdown}
  %\VignetteEncoding{UTF-8}
---

```{r setup, include=FALSE}
library(wsMed)
options(width = 80)
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>", cache = FALSE)
data(example_data)

# Read the seven reported indirect effects/contrasts from each stored report.
read_memore <- function(file) {
  text <- readLines(system.file("extdata", file, package = "wsMed"), warn = FALSE)
  rows <- grep("^(Ind[123]|Total|\\(C[123]\\)) +[-.0-9]", text, value = TRUE)
  stopifnot(length(rows) == 7L)
  tab <- read.table(text = rows, col.names = c("key", "estimate", "se", "low", "high"))
  list(text = text, table = tab)
}
parallel_reference <- read_memore("OUTPUT.txt")
serial_reference <- read_memore("OUTPUT2.txt")

compare_indirect <- function(result, reference, ids, labels) {
  effects <- wsmed_effects(result, type = "all")
  tab <- as.data.frame(effects)
  terms <- tab$term[match(ids, tab$effect)]
  pairs <- list(c(1, 2), c(1, 3), c(2, 3))
  contrasts <- lapply(pairs, function(pair) setNames(c(1, -1), terms[pair]))
  names(contrasts) <- c("C1", "C2", "C3")
  ct <- as.data.frame(wsmed_contrasts(effects, contrasts))
  ordered <- tab[match(c(ids, "total_indirect"), tab$effect), ]
  ws <- rbind(ordered[c("estimate", "std.error", "conf.low", "conf.high")],
              ct[c("estimate", "std.error", "conf.low", "conf.high")])
  ref <- reference$table
  # The stored report rounds point estimates to four decimal places.
  stopifnot(!anyNA(ws), max(abs(ref$estimate - ws$estimate)) < 0.000051,
            all(tab$interval == "percentile"), all(tab$n.valid == 5000L))
  ci <- function(lo, hi) sprintf("[%.4f, %.4f]", lo, hi)
  data.frame(
    Effect = c(labels, "Total indirect", "Ind1 - Ind2", "Ind1 - Ind3", "Ind2 - Ind3"),
    `MEMORE estimate` = sprintf("%.4f", ref$estimate),
    `wsMed estimate` = sprintf("%.4f", ws$estimate),
    `MEMORE 95% CI` = ci(ref$low, ref$high),
    `wsMed 95% CI` = ci(ws$conf.low, ws$conf.high),
    check.names = FALSE
  )
}
```

## What is compared?

These examples compare unstandardized indirect effects and their contrasts
in a parallel and a serial model. They use the package's simulated
`example_data`, not the empirical manuscript dataset. The MEMORE 3.0 reports
are stored SPSS output; they are not rerun when this page is built. The wsMed
analyses below are executed when the tutorial is rebuilt.

| Setting | Matched choice |
|---|---|
| Data | The same 100 complete simulated participant rows |
| Direction | Condition 2 minus condition 1 for every pair |
| Model | The same mediator ordering and directed connections |
| Covariates and moderator | None; MEMORE does not support covariates |
| Scale | Unstandardized effects |
| Indirect-effect inference | 5,000 participant bootstrap samples; 95% percentile CIs |

The default wsMed analysis uses Monte Carlo inference. Here we explicitly
request bootstrap inference and 5,000 replicates to match the method and sample
count in the stored reports. The two programs do not use identical resamples,
so matching settings does not imply identical CI endpoints.

**Why focus on indirect effects?** In these MEMORE reports, total and direct
effects use regression-based SEs and *t* intervals. The wsMed bootstrap report
uses bootstrap SEs and percentile CIs for all effects. Thus the indirect-effect
intervals below have a common interpretation; the total- and direct-effect
intervals should not be treated as equivalent output. Contrasts are calculated
within each bootstrap sample, retaining dependence between indirect effects.

## Parallel mediation

The three mediators are A, B and C; the outcome is D. There are no directed
connections among mediators.

```{r parallel}
result1 <- wsMed(
  data = example_data,
  M_C1 = c("A1", "B1", "C1"),
  M_C2 = c("A2", "B2", "C2"),
  Y_C1 = "D1",
  Y_C2 = "D2",
  ci_method = "bootstrap",
  bootstrap = 5000
)
print(result1)
```

The table aligns specific pathways, their total, and the same signed contrasts:

```{r parallel-comparison, echo=FALSE}
parallel_comparison <- compare_indirect(
  result1, parallel_reference,
  c("indirect_1", "indirect_2", "indirect_3"),
  c("Ind1: A -> D", "Ind2: B -> D", "Ind3: C -> D")
)
knitr::kable(parallel_comparison, align = c("l", "r", "r", "r", "r"))
```

<details>
<summary>Full stored MEMORE 3.0 parallel-model report</summary>

```{r parallel-report, echo=FALSE, results='asis'}
cat("```text\n", paste(parallel_reference$text, collapse = "\n"), "\n```\n", sep = "")
```

</details>

## Serial mediation

The two mediators are A and B; the outcome is C. `form = "CN"` adds the
A-to-B connection, matching the stored MEMORE serial specification. The
third indirect effect now follows A → B → C.

```{r serial}
result2 <- wsMed(
  data = example_data,
  M_C1 = c("A1", "B1"),
  M_C2 = c("A2", "B2"),
  Y_C1 = "C1",
  Y_C2 = "C2",
  form = "CN",
  ci_method = "bootstrap",
  bootstrap = 5000
)
print(result2)
```

```{r serial-comparison, echo=FALSE}
serial_comparison <- compare_indirect(
  result2, serial_reference,
  c("indirect_1", "indirect_2", "indirect_1_2"),
  c("Ind1: A -> C", "Ind2: B -> C", "Ind3: A -> B -> C")
)
knitr::kable(serial_comparison, align = c("l", "r", "r", "r", "r"))
```

<details>
<summary>Full stored MEMORE 3.0 serial-model report</summary>

```{r serial-report, echo=FALSE, results='asis'}
cat("```text\n", paste(serial_reference$text, collapse = "\n"), "\n```\n", sep = "")
```

</details>

## Reading the comparison

The indirect-effect and contrast point estimates agree to the four decimal
places available in the stored MEMORE reports. Percentile CI endpoints can
differ because of the bootstrap resamples and quantile conventions.

Centering also matters in the serial example. MEMORE recenters mediator
averages in each bootstrap sample, while wsMed resamples variables centered
during data preparation. This can change downstream-mediator intercepts and
their indirect-effect draws, even with identical resamples. Interpret the
serial intervals in light of this additional implementation difference.

Matching point estimates establishes agreement for these fitted models; it does
not by itself validate interval coverage or other model specifications.

The comparison does not establish agreement for standardized or moderated
effects. Their definitions and scale conventions need separate checks; see
[Standardized effects](StandardizedModeratedMediation.html). For a first analysis
with the default wsMed settings, use [Get started](GetStarted.html).
